# Kavacha patch 0057 — the widget host: dashboard widgets, and the `widget`
# and `panel` marketplace component types (REMAINING_WORK.md §3
# "Customization": "Widget host — blocks the reserved `widget` and `panel`
# marketplace component types, and blocks user-defined dashboards"; ADR 0010).
#
# ADR 0010 reserved both types and shipped no installer for either, recording
# "there is no sidebar-widget or tool-panel host engine yet". This is that
# engine — one missing piece that three separate follow-ups were queued behind,
# so it is deliberately the smallest thing that genuinely unblocks all three
# rather than a dashboard framework.
#
# THE CONTRACT is { id, name, render(doc, win) -> Node }, and that is all of
# it. A widget returns a Node and the host places it: no hook into chrome
# layout, no persistent process, no way to run at startup. That narrowness is
# the point — `widget` is a type the MARKETPLACE can install, so the surface a
# third-party component gets has to be one that cannot be used to reshape the
# browser.
#
# WHERE THEY GO: a dashboard panel, not a docked sidebar column. A docked
# region has to fight Zen's chrome layout in both tab orientations, which is a
# lot of risk for a surface nobody has asked to be permanent. The panel is a
# real, arrangeable dashboard today, and the widget contract would not have to
# change to add a docked mode later.
#
# ARRANGEMENT lives in the layout document (`widgets: ["id", ...]`), because
# kavacha-layout.json already IS "how the user has arranged their browser" and
# already round-trips with about:studio and with `layout` components. An absent
# list means "the built-ins in their declared order", so an existing profile
# gets a working dashboard with no migration, and unknown ids are dropped
# rather than surfaced as errors — a widget can vanish simply because its
# component was uninstalled, which is not a fault.
#
# WHAT THE TWO TYPES NOW MEAN: a `widget` component adds one card; a `panel`
# component sets the whole arrangement, which is what a curated set is and
# what makes it one click. Both go through the same
# register/unregisterBySource shape the command registry already uses, so an
# uninstall drops a component's widgets as a group.
#
# The three built-ins read only data Kavacha already holds locally — Spaces,
# the active Space's note (through KavachaMarkdown, which builds nodes and
# never parses HTML, because this is chrome), and its snapshots. No widget
# performs network I/O and the host offers no way to. A widget that throws is
# skipped with its error logged rather than taking the dashboard down, which
# matters specifically because third-party components can be widgets.
#
# Why a patch: a new chrome module needs a moz.build entry and a ZenStartup
# init, and the marketplace's type taxonomy and installer are Kavacha chrome
# sources.
#
# Touches: src/zen/common/sys/KavachaWidgetHost.sys.mjs (new),
#          src/zen/common/moz.build, src/zen/common/modules/ZenStartup.mjs,
#          src/zen/common/sys/KavachaLayoutEngine.sys.mjs (widgets field),
#          src/zen/common/sys/KavachaMarketplace.sys.mjs (types + installers),
#          src/zen/common/sys/KavachaCommandRegistry.sys.mjs (Dashboard),
#          src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-menu.inc.css,
#          locales/en-US/browser/browser/zen-command-palette.ftl.
# Applies on top of 0056.
